  "deploy": [
    {
      "order": 1,
      "phase": "configure",
      "module": "authelia",
      "id": "accounts",
      "title": "Review the sign-in accounts",
      "instructions": "Sign-in is on (`[selection.platform] identity`): Authelia checks every API and UI route on the\ngateway's shared listener. Browsers get its login page; API clients send basic auth.\n\nThe accounts live in `modules/authelia/users.yml`. It starts with one demo account, username\n`demo` with password `authelia`. Change or remove that account before you share the\nenvironment. To add, change, or remove users:\n\n1. Edit `modules/authelia/users.yml`. Each user is one block under `users:`. Copy the `demo`\n   block, then change the name, `displayname`, `email`, `groups`, and `password`.\n2. Hash each password (Authelia stores argon2id hashes, not plain text), then paste the printed\n   `$argon2id$…` digest in as the user's `password`:\n\n   ```bash\n   docker run --rm authelia/authelia:latest \\\n     authelia crypto hash generate argon2 --password 'choose-a-password'\n   ```\n3. If the stack is already running, restart Authelia so it rereads the file:\n   `docker compose restart authelia`.\n\nThe services behind the gateway receive a user's `email` and `displayname` as the\n`Remote-Email` and `Remote-Name` headers, and `groups` as `Remote-Groups`. With policy on, the\ndefault policy lets any signed-in user read and lets the `writers` or `admins` group write.\n\n`trestle env render` writes `users.yml` only when it doesn't exist yet, so your edits survive\nre-renders. To start over from the demo account, delete the file and render again."
    },
    {
      "order": 2,
      "phase": "configure",
      "module": "unity-catalog-rs",
      "id": "kek",
      "title": "Set Unity Catalog's key-encryption key",
      "instructions": "Unity Catalog seals the credentials it stores with a key-encryption key, read from\n`UC_SERVER_KEK` (32 bytes, base64) in the environment of the process that starts the stack.\nCompose passes it through to the server; it is never written into this directory, and without\nit the server refuses to start. Generate one once and keep it: credentials sealed under it\ncan't be read with another.\n\n```sh\nexport UC_SERVER_KEK=\"$(openssl rand -base64 32)\"\n```"
    },
    {
      "order": 3,
      "phase": "configure",
      "module": "mlflow",
      "id": "kek",
      "title": "Set the AI Gateway's key passphrase",
      "instructions": "MLflow seals the provider keys and tokens its AI Gateway stores with a passphrase, read from\n`MLFLOW_CRYPTO_KEK_PASSPHRASE` in the environment of the process that starts the stack. Compose\npasses it through to the server; it is never written into this directory, and without it the\nstack doesn't start. Generate one once and keep it: keys sealed under it can't be read with\nanother.\n\n```sh\nexport MLFLOW_CRYPTO_KEK_PASSPHRASE=\"$(openssl rand -base64 32)\"\n```\n\nWith trestle's state store, keep it in the keychain instead (`<target>` is the workspace or\nenvironment, as for any `trestle var set`):\n`openssl rand -base64 32 | trestle var set <target> --secret MLFLOW_CRYPTO_KEK_PASSPHRASE`.\n\nAn environment made before the passphrase was required sealed its keys under\n`lakehouse-dev-kek`: set that value to keep them, or delete the gateway's secrets in the MLflow\nUI and let the next start store them again."
    },
    {
      "order": 4,
      "phase": "configure",
      "id": "local-credentials",
      "title": "Set the services' own credentials",
      "instructions": "The services the stack runs use these credentials. Unless `secrets.env` sets them, they are fixed development values, the same in every environment.\n\n| Credential | Name in `secrets.env` |\n| --- | --- |\n| Postgres superuser password | `LOCAL_POSTGRES_PASSWORD` |\n| RustFS root access key | `LOCAL_RUSTFS_ACCESS_KEY` |\n| RustFS root secret key | `LOCAL_RUSTFS_SECRET_KEY` |\n| Authelia session secret | `LOCAL_AUTHELIA_SESSION_SECRET` |\n| Authelia storage encryption key | `LOCAL_AUTHELIA_STORAGE_ENCRYPTION_KEY` |\n| Authelia identity-verification JWT secret | `LOCAL_AUTHELIA_JWT_SECRET` |\n\nTo replace them with random ones, add them to `secrets.env` and render again. Rendering writes each value into every file that uses it (`.env`, `modules/*/.env/`, `modules/*/secrets/`, all git-ignored):\n\n```bash\ncat >> secrets.env <<EOF\nLOCAL_POSTGRES_PASSWORD=$(openssl rand -hex 32)\nLOCAL_RUSTFS_ACCESS_KEY=$(openssl rand -hex 10)\nLOCAL_RUSTFS_SECRET_KEY=$(openssl rand -hex 20)\nLOCAL_AUTHELIA_SESSION_SECRET=$(openssl rand -hex 32)\nLOCAL_AUTHELIA_STORAGE_ENCRYPTION_KEY=$(openssl rand -hex 32)\nLOCAL_AUTHELIA_JWT_SECRET=$(openssl rand -hex 32)\nEOF\ntrestle env render .\n```\n\nDo this before the first start. Services keep the credentials they first started with in their persisted data (under the data root, `.data/` by default), so a later change also needs that data removed, or the credential changed inside the service."
    },
    {
      "order": 5,
      "phase": "start",
      "id": "up",
      "title": "Start the stack",
      "instructions": "Start every service and wait until each one is healthy:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose up -d --wait\n```\n\nThe gateway then serves the stack at <https://platform.localhost:9080>. [LAYOUT.md](LAYOUT.md) lists every route and address."
    },
    {
      "order": 6,
      "phase": "verify",
      "module": "mlflow",
      "id": "traces",
      "title": "Find the AI Gateway's traces",
      "instructions": "Each call through the AI Gateway (`/ai-gateway/…` at the gateway, see [LAYOUT.md](LAYOUT.md)) is\nrecorded as a trace in MLflow. The traces of an endpoint go to its own experiment, named\n`gateway/<endpoint>` (e.g. `gateway/claude-opus-5-5`), not to `Default`: open it under\n**Experiments**, then **Traces**. The `mlflow-gateway-init` job creates every endpoint with\ntracing on, and turns it back on at each start if it was switched off.\n\nThe server logs `Failed to send trace to MLflow backend: When an mlflow-artifacts URI was\nsupplied, …` for each call. That warning is harmless: MLflow has already stored the trace's\nspans in its database and only fails to write an extra copy to artifact storage."
    },
    {
      "order": 7,
      "phase": "verify",
      "id": "trust",
      "title": "Trust the gateway's local certificate authority",
      "instructions": "The gateway serves HTTPS with a certificate from a CA minted for this environment. Copy it out, then add it to your browser or pass it to `curl --cacert`:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose cp gateway-certs:/certs/ca.pem .\n```"
    }
  ]
}
